  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 447 AM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places low over northwest Ontario with\ncold front dropping southwest through Minnesota into South\nDakota. Behind the cold front, high pressure continues to settle\nin over southern Saskatchewan into eastern Montana and far\nwestern North Dakota. Upper level analysis places high centered\nover the Four Corners region, with ridge over the western CONUS\nand trough pushing through the Northern Plains into the Midwest.\nQuiet weather remains over our area in the wake of the\naforementioned front.\n\nFor today, a quiet and cooler day is on tap with highs generally\naround 5 to 10 degrees below average as cooler air settles\nthrough. Quiet weather continues into tonight.\n\nOn Saturday, West Coast ridge broadens a bit to the east, and\ncooler airmass gets shunted a bit to the east. This will result\nin a bit of a temperature gradient over our area, with highs\nmainly in the upper 70s over the Turtle Mountains, while\nclimbing into the lower to mid 90s over southwest into parts of\nsouth central North Dakota.\n\nSunday will see the start of a bit of a transition in the\npattern as a few short waves sliding through the flow break the\nridge to the west down a bit, with flow becoming more west- \nnorthwest which continues into mid-week. This will result in \nhigh temperatures being near to somewhat below average. In \naddition, a variety of waves moving through the flow will bring \na near daily chance (~20 to 50 percent) for precipitation.",
